---
intent: >
  Eliminate the full-screen drill-in mode and render all detail content in the
  right panel while the tree stays permanently visible. Add Tab-based body
  section cycling and an [e] editor escape hatch.
---

## Plan

### 1. Remove full-screen drill-in rendering from App

- [x] In `src/cli/App/index.tsx`, remove the early-return block (currently lines 298-311) that renders `<DrillInPanel>` as a full-screen `<Box flexDirection="column">` when `mode === "drill-in" && drilledFile`. The two-panel `<Box flexDirection="row">` layout at line 313 must render unconditionally — the tree panel is always visible regardless of mode. Do NOT remove the DrillInPanel import yet (that happens in step 3). After this change, entering drill-in mode will show the tree + the existing DetailPanel (which only knows preview mode), so drill-in will temporarily render as a preview. This is intentional — step 2 adds the drill-in rendering to DetailPanel.

### 2. Merge drill-in content into DetailPanel

- [x] 2a. In `src/cli/DetailPanel/index.tsx`, extend the props interface to accept: `mode: "preview" | "drill-in"`, `sections: BodySection[]`, `focusedSection: number`, `expandedSection: number | null` (single currently-expanded section index, or null when all collapsed), and `drilledFilePath: string | null` (shown as the panel title during drill-in). Import `BodySection` from `@/types/index.js`. When `mode === "preview"`, render the existing summary preview unchanged. When `mode === "drill-in"`, render the full frontmatter card: scope as a heading, full intent paragraph (not truncated), and side-by-side outcomes in bordered regions. Move the `OutcomeList` and `OutcomesDisplay` helper functions from `src/cli/DrillInPanel/index.tsx` into `DetailPanel/index.tsx` above the default export. These are purely presentational sub-renders — they stay in the same file per the single-helper exception.
- [x] 2b. In DetailPanel's drill-in mode, render the body section list below the frontmatter card. Use the `expandedSection` prop (a single `number | null`) to control which section is expanded — only one section at a time, or none. The focused section (matching `focusedSection` index) is highlighted with bold cyan text. Each section heading shows a collapsed indicator (`"▸"`) or expanded indicator (`"▾"`). When expanded, the section content renders below its heading. When collapsed, the section shows an optional summary annotation (e.g., `"(2 paragraphs)"`).
- [x] 2c. Add a drill-in footer with hint text: `[esc] back  [tab] next section  [e] open in editor`. The preview mode footer stays as-is (no `[tab]` or `[e]` hints).

### 3. Delete DrillInPanel

- [x] Delete `src/cli/DrillInPanel/index.tsx` (and the `DrillInPanel/` folder). Remove the `DrillInPanel` import from `src/cli/App/index.tsx`.

### 4. Rewire App state and DetailPanel props

- [x] In `src/cli/App/index.tsx`, change the `drillExpanded` state from `useState<Set<number>>` to `useState<number | null>(null)` and rename it to `expandedSection` / `setExpandedSection`. Update the `drillIntoFile` callback to reset `setExpandedSection(null)` instead of `setDrillExpanded(new Set())`. Pass the following new props to `<DetailPanel>`: `mode` (derive from the existing `mode` state: `mode === "drill-in" ? "drill-in" : "preview"`), `sections` (from `drillData?.sections ?? []`), `focusedSection` (from `drillFocused`), `expandedSection` (the new state), and `drilledFilePath` (from `drilledFile?.relativePath ?? null`). The `<DetailPanel>` now always renders inside the right-panel `<Box>` — it shows preview when `mode === "tree"` and drill-in content when `mode === "drill-in"`.

### 5. Implement Tab section cycling in App

- [x] In `src/cli/App/index.tsx`, modify the drill-in keyboard handler. When `mode === "drill-in"` and Tab is pressed: cycle `expandedSection` forward through the body sections. The cycle is: `null -> 0 -> 1 -> ... -> (sectionCount - 1) -> null -> 0 -> ...`. This means pressing Tab when no section is expanded opens the first section; pressing Tab when the last section is expanded collapses all; pressing Tab again reopens the first. Remove the existing Enter-to-toggle logic in drill-in mode (Enter should be a no-op in drill-in). Keep Up/Down for moving `drillFocused` between section headings. Tab in tree mode continues to toggle deep/shallow view (no change to that path).

### 6. Implement [e] editor escape hatch in App

- [x] In `src/cli/App/index.tsx`, add `[e]` keypress handling in drill-in mode. When the user presses `e` and `drilledFile` is non-null: resolve the editor command from `process.env.VISUAL ?? process.env.EDITOR ?? "code"`. Call `spawnSync(editor, [drilledFile.path], { stdio: "inherit" })` to hand terminal control to the editor. Import `spawnSync` from `node:child_process` at the top of the file. After the editor process exits, the TUI resumes automatically since Ink redraws on the next render cycle.

### 7. Update existing drill-in integration tests

- [x] In `src/cli/App/index.test.tsx`, update the following tests to assert the tree stays visible during drill-in:
  - **"switches to drill-in mode on Enter"** — after drilling in, assert the frame contains BOTH `"Intent Tree"` (tree panel header) AND the drill-in content (`"scope:"`, `"Desired Outcomes"`, `"Undesired Outcomes"`). Also assert dir entries (`. /`) are still visible.
  - **"Escape in drill-in mode returns to tree view"** — after pressing Escape, verify the right panel reverts to preview mode. Assert the frame contains outcome counts (`"desired ("` or `"undesired ("`) rather than full outcome text.
  - **"Enter on file inside expanded dir triggers drill-in"** — same as above: assert tree + drill-in content coexist in the frame.
  - **"drill-in mode: Up/Down moves section focus and Enter expands"** — this test currently uses Enter to expand sections. Rewrite it to use Tab instead: Tab expands sections one at a time. Assert that pressing Tab expands the first section, pressing Tab again collapses it and expands the second.
  - **"drill-in mode: Up arrow moves section focus, Enter expands"** — rewrite similarly: focus movement with Up/Down is unchanged, but expansion is now via Tab, not Enter.

### 8. Add new tests for Tab section cycling

- [x] In `src/cli/App/index.test.tsx`, add:
  - **"drill-in mode: Tab cycles through body sections one at a time"**: Drill into a file. Press Tab — first section expands (assert expanded indicator `"▾"` appears and section content is visible). Press Tab — first section collapses, second section expands. Press Tab — all sections collapse (both show collapsed indicator `"▸"`). Press Tab — first section expands again (wraps around).
  - **"drill-in mode: only one section expanded at a time"**: Drill in, press Tab to expand section 0, assert section 0 has `"▾"` indicator. Press Tab again — assert section 0 now has `"▸"` AND section 1 has `"▾"`. At no point should two sections show `"▾"` simultaneously.

### 9. Add test for [e] editor escape hatch

- [x] In `src/cli/App/index.test.tsx`, add `vi.mock("node:child_process", ...)` at the top to mock `spawnSync`. Add a test: **"drill-in mode: [e] opens file in editor"**: drill into a file, press `e`, assert `spawnSync` was called once with an array containing the drilled file's absolute path (`"/mock/.aide"`) and `{ stdio: "inherit" }` in the options.

### 10. Add test for tree visibility during drill-in

- [x] In `src/cli/App/index.test.tsx`, add: **"tree panel remains visible during drill-in"**: drill into a file, assert the frame contains `"Intent Tree"`, `". /"`, `"src/tools/init/"`, and `"scope:"` all in the same frame. This explicitly pins the no-full-screen-takeover contract from the spec's undesired outcomes.

## Decisions

**1. DetailPanel absorbs DrillInPanel rather than keeping two swappable components.**
The spec says the right panel is "the only detail surface" — it transitions between preview and drill-in views within the same panel. Making DetailPanel handle both modes via a `mode` prop keeps the App orchestrator simple (one detail component to render, one set of props to wire) and mirrors the spec's mental model that there is one panel with two states, not two panels that swap. The `OutcomeList` and `OutcomesDisplay` sub-components move into DetailPanel as private helpers under the single-helper exception — they are purely presentational sub-renders with no independent reuse. Traced to: spec strategy ("the right panel is the only detail surface"), playbook (component architecture, orchestrator/helper pattern).

**2. `expandedSection: number | null` replaces `Set<number>` for one-at-a-time cycling.**
The spec says "expanding one section at a time while collapsing the previous." A Set allows multiple sections expanded simultaneously, which contradicts the spec. A single nullable index enforces the one-at-a-time constraint by construction. Tab increments the index (with null as the "all collapsed" state between the last section and wrap-around to first), which is simpler than toggling Set membership. Traced to: spec strategy ("cycle-through model — expanding one section at a time while collapsing the previous").

**3. Tab serves dual role: deep/shallow toggle in tree mode, section cycling in drill-in mode.**
Tab is already bound to deep/shallow toggle in tree mode. The spec says "Tab cycles through body sections in the detail panel." These contexts are mutually exclusive (the `mode` state is either `"tree"` or `"drill-in"`), so Tab can serve both roles without conflict. The keyboard handler already dispatches on `mode`. Traced to: spec strategy ("keyboard navigation follows terminal conventions").

**4. Editor spawned synchronously with `spawnSync` + `stdio: "inherit"`.**
The editor must take over the terminal (stdin/stdout). `spawnSync` with `stdio: "inherit"` is the standard Node.js pattern — it blocks the event loop while the editor runs, which is correct because the TUI should be frozen during editing. The fallback chain is `$VISUAL` -> `$EDITOR` -> `"code"`. We do not fall back to `vi` — `code` (VS Code) is a safer default for the target audience (developers using an MCP server are likely using VS Code). Traced to: spec strategy ("a keypress opens the raw file directly in the user's editor").

**5. No changes to TreePanel, flattenTree, buildTreeData, findPrimaryIntent, or any tree data pipeline.**
The refactor is entirely within App's rendering path (which panel renders what, when) and DetailPanel's content. The tree data pipeline, folder expansion, search filtering, and primary intent resolution are all unaffected — they already work correctly from the previous plan's implementation. This keeps the blast radius minimal and avoids regressions in tested, stable code. Traced to: playbook (horizontal isolation, no scope creep).

**6. Steps 1-4 are tightly sequenced; steps 7-10 (tests) depend on 1-6 but are independent of each other.**
Steps 1-6 form a dependency chain: step 1 removes the full-screen path, step 2 adds drill-in capability to DetailPanel, step 3 cleans up the dead component, step 4 wires the new props, steps 5-6 add new keyboard behaviors. Test steps 7-10 can be executed in any order after the code changes land, but each is an independent step so a fresh agent gets clear scope. Traced to: plan.aide spec ("each numbered step is a unit of delegation").
